F213 — a constant name is global, and the last declaration wins

THE REAL FIX. Supersedes F213-part1-enclosing-scope-key.patch (which it contains) and replaces
F213-both-parts.patch, which fixed 13 rows and broke 17.

Part 1 registered the enclosing-scope key so `Alpha::Low` resolves to its own declaration, and fixed 42
of 55 rows. The remaining 13 were the bare spelling:

    static constexpr uint64 Threshold = 5;
    namespace Port { enum Limits { Threshold = 55 }; }
    state.mut().fromFile = Threshold;    // clang 5, TypeScript 55

A bare key is not a declaration — it is how a using-directive reaches a namespaced name — so several
declarations compete for one slot. Both collectConstant and collectEnum took it unconditionally and
deleted the other kind's entry with it, so whichever was collected last won.

The rejected part 2 stopped an enum from evicting a `constexpr`. That is precedence by **kind**, which
is the same bug mirrored: it made a constexpr beat a later enum member outright and turned 17
`LogPayloadWithIdField` rows red. This decides by **scope** instead, which is what C++ does: the
nearest declaration owns the bare name, and equal scope keeps last-writer-wins so a contract still
shadows a qpi.h constant of the same name. Ownership is recorded once per declaration in
`bareNameScope`, and the cross-kind deletes follow it, so neither side can evict a nearer declaration.

Order-independent: the file-scope constant wins whether it is collected before or after the namespaced
enum.

Measured: TwinEnum both backends identical on all ten fields. The `namespaces` family is 831/831 on
value rows (27 stale pins for F209/F217 remain). **The `logging` family is 306/306** — the 17 rows part
2 broke are untouched, which is the difference between deciding by kind and deciding by scope.
Suite 1085/0.

